Z||000002||Joe Torma||12-30-2001||06:15 PM||||Um, are you turning it in the right direction? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">  Make sure to keep the ratchet square on that bolt.<P>Liquid wrench or equiv may help too.<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero STS||12-30-2001||10:37 PM||||try using a punch and strike the end of the bolt to loosen it||||reg||1
Z||000004||West Coast Fiero||12-30-2001||10:43 PM||||you will need a torch to heat up the bolt before any penetrant or T50's will have any affect<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||12-30-2001||11:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>you will need a torch to heat up the bolt before any penetrant or T50's will have any affect<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Be extremely careful with the torch, though, only heat the area directly around the bolt.  You don't want to heat up your brake fluid (flammable, I believe), or heat up the caliper enough to warp/melt the inner piston seal (square-cut o-ring)<P>Let pentrating oil (your favorite brand) soak over night.  Throw a lot on there, wait a little, and put more on and then let the last application sit overnight.  Rap it with a hammer to break some of the corrosion loose.  When you get them out and are ready to put everything back together, don't forget to:<P>1. Regrease (axle/bearing grease) the slide bolts and the inner slides, make sure they move very easily.<BR>2. Clean up all your penetrating oil with brake cleaner.  Especially the rotor surface (both side)<BR>3. Not to over-tighten the slide bolts!  You don't want to have to go through all this again!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Kasdan||12-30-2001||11:26 PM||||Its wierd....because all the other calipers were easy...and its just this one which is hard....and (i think)the bolt turns a little before breaking the bits (may be the bit itself twsting, they all are like that)  could the bolt be cross-threaded?  do i need new calipers?  how much would (total) calipers and rotors (generic autozone brand) be for an 86 GT?  email me if u know the total....mtyler14@yahoo.com<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan, 1986 GT<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kassig2.gif">                    <BR>Aftermarket Power Steering, Rebuilt 2.8<BR>http://www.geocities.com/mtyler14/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000007||Kasdan||12-30-2001||11:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>Get better quality T-50 bits...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got 2 generic "taiwan" ones from a friend, the last one i bought, it was an "oly-pro" chrome canadium bit, with a lifetime warranty...ive never had one of that brand break before...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Joe Torma||12-30-2001||11:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Kasdan:<BR><B>...could the bolt be cross-threaded?  do i need new calipers?...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If its cross threaded, its crossthreaded into the whole assembly where the bearing and brakes bolt to.||||reg||1
